00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
I haven't quite run out of things to say,

122
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
And we're going to be talking about the roles that we play

123
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
as scout leaders- not our volunteer positions,

124
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
but some of the roles that we end up playing.

125
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
And I have a little scout master's minute

126
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
that's intended for scout masters, about some of the things

127
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
that I learned from a very experienced coach,

128
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
And I hope you will find it all useful.

129
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So let's get started, shall we

130
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Scout mastership in seven minutes or less.

131
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
If I ask you what you do as an adult involved in scouting,

132
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
you're going to tell me what your volunteer position is.

133
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
If somebody asks me that, I would say I'm a scoutmaster.

134
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
You might say you're a scoutmaster,

135
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
assistant scoutmaster, cubmaster,

136
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
den leader, troop, committee person,

137
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
round table something or other

138
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
counsel, one of those deals,

139
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
You know. that is the title,

140
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
the volunteer position that you occupy.

141
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
The other thing that's a little more subtle

142
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
is we also play roles within those positions.

143
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Now, this isn't just a purely academic exercise

144
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
where I get to use a lot of $9 college words,

145
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
But I think it's important to differentiate

146
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
between the volunteer position you have

147
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and the different roles that you play,

148
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Because when you're playing a role,

149
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
you should be kind of cognizant of the elements

150
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,000
and skills that affect that role.

151
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:50,000
So are you confused? yet

152
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
You have a volunteer position.

153
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Within that position, you play several different roles

154
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
and there's elements and skills

155
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
that affect the way that you play that role.

156
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
So, for instance, as a scoutmaster, at times

157
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
you may end up being a manager, an administrator,

158
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
a coach, a mentor, a counselor.

159
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Do you understand what I'm saying?

160
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
You're going to play different roles.

161
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So what exactly roles are there?

162
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Well, the ones that I just said.

163
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
let's look at those real quick and just think about that.

164
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Managers are usually the people that keep the plate spinning.

165
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
They have to marshal resources and dispense them

166
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
in as efficient a manner as possible.

167
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
They keep abreast of developments

168
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
and they incorporate them into their strategies.

169
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
They're managing things.

170
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Administrators, on the other hand,

171
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
are ones that have to interpret and apply policies.

172
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
They make judgments and issue opinions.

173
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Their authority is broad and decisive

174
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
because they've got to be able to administer

175
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
the things that they're responsible for,

176
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
And a manager and administrator is a different role.

177
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
A coach: what do coaches do?

178
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Well, coaches detect and develop potential.

179
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
They do that in teams and in individuals.

180
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
They draw out the best qualities,

181
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
They define goals and game plans.

182
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
They oversee the practice and mastering of skills.

183
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
They know the rules and they require

184
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
that the rules be followed and they advocate for their team.

185
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
That's what coaches do,

186
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
A little bit different than that administrator role

187
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
or that manager role.

188
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Scout volunteers play the role of mentor and counselor. at times

189
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,000
We bring a holistic approach to individual growth in advance.

190
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
but rather than just- you know- the concerns of a group,

191
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
we're looking at individual growth.

192
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
We seek to understand the goals of our individual scouts

193
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
and help them achieve them.

194
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
We want to guide them past obstacles

195
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and help them overcome difficulties.

196
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Unlike managers or administrators,

197
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
mentors don't have a whole lot of authority

198
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
to make changes or demand that things be done.

199
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Mentors are there exercising empathy

200
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
and making some suggestions and things like that.

201
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
They're really on an individual's side.

202
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Every once in a while, we have to occupy

203
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
the role of a disciplinarian.

204
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
We have to maintain established standards of behavior.

205
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
We use the rulebook, of course, as a means of positive

206
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and the negative reinforcement.

207
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Every once in a while, we play the role of leader.

208
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
Well, what do leaders do?

209
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Leaders make decisions. Leaders set goals.

210
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
They exercise authority.

211
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
They bring people together under a common vision.

212
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Leaders have a great deal of authority

213
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
to cause change or to make progress.

214
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Sometimes we find ourselves in the role of an advisor,

215
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
and an advisor uses their kind of unbiased expertise

216
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
to offer non-binding guidance.

217
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Well, let me give you some advice.

218
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
I'll advise you to do this. It's not an instruction.

219
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
They're advising.

220
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
They're knowledgeable and experienced in their field,

221
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
but they're really just consulting participants

222
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,000
with little to no authority in the matter.

223
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Teachers and instructors guide people

224
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
to acquire a given set of skills or knowledge,

225
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
and we occupy that role at times.

226
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
So there's these different roles that we play

227
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
within the context of a volunteer position.

228
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Well, what skills do you need

229
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
to be able to play those different roles?

230
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
One skill I think is really underrated is observation.

231
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Observers, by definition, aren't participants.

232
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
They're passive onlookers, They're spectators.

233
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
They're students of activity,

234
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
They're students of relationships.

235
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Developing the skills of a disinterested,

236
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
unbiased observer

237
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
can really open a whole new way of approaching problems.

238
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
It can open a whole new way of evaluating

239
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
the effectiveness of programs

240
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
and learning about the relationships

241
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and playing a given situation.

242
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
I like to think of myself sometimes

243
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
as an observer, kind of like an anthropologist,

244
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
observing the behavior of another culture

245
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
or observing animals out in the field.

246
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
I try to approach observing totally free of bilis,

247
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
as if I am looking at something for the first time

248
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and I don't have any knowledge of it at all,

249
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
And that's usually when I end up learning things.

250
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Knowledge is an asset and learning is a skill

251
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
that we need to have in these different roles

252
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
that we exercise in the context of a volunteer position,

253
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
And we get this knowledge by observing

254
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and reading and listening and participating and training.

255
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
All these things are going to build knowledge.

256
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
We can always learn more, though,

257
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
and evolve to continuously strive

258
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
to learn better about our work.

259
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Vision is another important asset

260
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
and an important skill

261
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
to effectively playing these roles

262
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
within our volunteer positions,

263
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Helping coalesce the aspiration

264
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
and hopes and dreams

265
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
of the scouts that you serve

266
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and your fellow volunteers.

267
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
What potential is there in your group?

268
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Can you discover and realize?

269
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
That is a very important skill

270
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
to try and develop, to have some vision.

271
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Positive reinforcement-

272
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Now that doesn't sound like a skill, but it is

273
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Thinking about. you know the scouting way

274
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:25,000
of not using hazing or push-ups or punishments,

275
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
but to positively reinforce good behavior

276
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
to the exclusion of bad behavior.

277
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
It's a challenge, but it's something unique to scouting

278
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and it's something that we need to develop as a skill.

279
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
We also need to possess some skills

280
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
as teachers and instructors.

281
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
You need to understand how to help people learn,

282
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
how to inspire them to think for themselves.

283
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
So these are all important skills

284
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and assets to use.

285
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
There are some elements that are interesting to think about

286
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
in the context of these roles.

287
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
One of the elements is authority.

288
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Now, authority, by definition, is the power to command people to action,

289
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
the power to shape opinions and thought.

290
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
That's authority,

291
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And authority can be very positive if it's used properly

292
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
and it can be very negative if it's used improperly.

293
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
So authority is something for us to understand

294
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and to begin to understand in the roles that we play

295
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
as volunteer leaders.

296
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Responsibility is another crucial element to understand,

297
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,000
and it's one that I think usually people have a pretty good understanding of-

298
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:37,000
that. we're invested with an elemental trust for the guardianship of our scouts

299
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
and the places we go and the things we use,

300
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
and we're accountable for progress and development and change.

301
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
We're answerable for our actions and directions and decisions

302
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:53,000
And authority and responsibility. they are twins just about.

303
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
They go together everywhere.

304
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:59,000
If you have responsibility, usually that comes with a measure of authority

305
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
to be able to realize those responsibilities.

306
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
A couple of other elements that we don't often think of, probably,

307
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:10,000
but bias is one of them, and bias sounds very negative

308
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and I'm not necessarily interested in couching it in negative terms.

309
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
But you know we have to realize that just by being human beings,

310
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
we're going to have some biases.

311
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Just by being adults, we're going to have some biases.

312
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
We're going to have preconceived judgments,

313
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
and now these judgments may be irrational

314
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
or they may lack basis. in fact

315
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
They may be skewed by our own personal experiences

316
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and they may tend to make us act one way all the time instead of others.

317
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,000
And biases, you know, if you're biased in a good way, that's good.

318
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
If you're biased in a negative way, that's negative.

319
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
but we should understand that bias is an element

320
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
that affects the way we play these different roles.

321
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
Coercion is another element,

322
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:58,000
and coercion is something that we probably aren't always aware of,

323
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:03,000
especially when we're talking about this kind of imbalance of power

324
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
between adults and youth.

325
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
We can compel youth to actions

326
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
because we can leverage our power and knowledge.

327
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,000
We can compel them to act and choose

328
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
the way that we want them to choose,

329
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:22,000
by the force of our personality or argument or intimidation.

330
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
So coercion is important to understand.

331
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
So let's go back and look at this one more time.

332
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
You have a volunteer position.

333
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Within that volunteer position, you'll play several different roles.

334
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
You may play the role of manager.

335
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
you may play the role of administrator,

336
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
of coach, of mentor and counselor,

337
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
of disciplinarian, of leader, advisor, teacher or instructor.

338
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
The skills you'll be depending on

339
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
to be able to play those roles effectively

340
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
are the skills of observation-

341
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Another asset, another skill

342
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
is knowledge in the acquiring of knowledge,

343
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Observing and reading and listening and participating

344
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
in all the training that we can

345
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
to build our knowledge of our work in scouting.

346
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Another skill and another asset is vision.

347
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
We need to be able to coalesce the aspirations

348
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and hopes and dreams of our scouts into a plan.

349
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
We need to help them find their way.

350
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,000
We need to learn how to use positive reinforcement.

351
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:22,000
We need to be effective teachers and instructors.

352
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Remember the elements that affect these things:

353
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
The element of authority,

354
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
the element of responsibility, of bias and coercion.

355
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Now, once we understand the roles that we play

356
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
within our respective volunteer positions,

357
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
the skills and assets that are required

358
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
to be able to play these roles effectively,

359
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and the different elements that affect the way

360
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
that we play those roles, a lot of answers begin

361
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
to formulate themselves.

362
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
I'll give you an example.

363
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
I'll give you an example.

364
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
I'll meet with my patrol leaders council

365
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,000
before our next troop leading and I'll ask the senior patrol leader

366
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
how things are going. does he have everything that he needs?

367
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
and in that I am playing the role of kind of a manager

368
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
or an administrator.

369
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
I'm checking in with him to make sure that he's doing his job

370
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and make sure that he has the resources that he needs,

371
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
that he's playing by the rules.

372
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
Whatever issue may arise out of that,

373
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
if he's short on a plan

374
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
or if he doesn't have the resources that he needs,

375
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:26,000
well then I might turn into an advisor and a coach.

376
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
I may be asking him:

377
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
well, where are you going to find that particular resource?

378
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
What are you going to do to make that happen?

379
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,000
And so I've left the role of management and administration

380
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
and now I'm advising and coaching.

381
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:46,000
I need to watch out that I'm not letting coercion take over,

382
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,000
that I'm not trying to get him to do what I want him to do.

383
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:53,000
I'm trying to help him discover what he's trying to get- none-

384
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:58,000
and trying to help him discover the best way to make it happen.

385
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:01,000
So the meeting happens and there we are,

386
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
we're standing there for the opening

387
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,000
and a couple of the boys are just, you know,

388
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
lined up with their patrol and they're playing in the corner of the room

389
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and I might walk over very quietly and I might ask

390
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,000
what's happening now.

391
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Well, what role am I in?

392
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:18,000
I'm in that role of, maybe, the disciplinarian, you know,

393
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
where I have some authority to exercise

394
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and I can go over and I can say something to them.

395
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:27,000
And yeah, you know, I would like my youth leaders to do it,

396
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
but maybe in this instance, I'm the one who had to step in

397
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:31,000
and make that happen.

398
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
So I've been in the role of disciplinarian

399
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,000
for 30 seconds or whatever. and as the meeting proceeds,

400
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
I end up meeting with a couple of scouts

401
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and doing a scout master conference,

402
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
where I am in several different roles,

403
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
maybe as coach and mentor, maybe as administrator,

404
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
maybe as a manager in there,

405
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
And I'm thinking about responsibility and authority.

406
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
Well, what's my responsibility in this particular role

407
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:55,000
that I'm doing right now?

408
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,000
What's my responsibility as a mentor, for instance?

409
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:02,000
Is it to provide all the answers and to teach?

410
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
No, not necessarily.

411
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,000
It's to help develop in them the skills to find the answers,

412
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and not to issue them a set of directions,

413
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
but help them find direction.

414
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
How does bias affect that situation?

415
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Well, you know, maybe I've had a run-in

416
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
with this particular scout two or three meetings ago

417
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
where I had to be in the disciplinarian role.

418
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Well, should I be biased towards him at this point?

419
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
Should I base the way that I'm working with him now

420
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
on all my past experiences,

421
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Or should I kind of open my mind a little bit?

422
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
So I've done my scout master's conferences

423
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:38,000
and I'm off and I'm looking in on the meeting room

424
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
and I'm just kind of looking around

425
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and the patrols are meeting right now

426
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and I'm observing.

427
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,000
I'm not really interested in stepping in

428
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,000
and making changes,

429
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:51,000
I'm not interested in managing or administering anything.

430
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
All I want to do is observe for a few minutes

431
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
just to see what's going on.

432
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
And then the meeting closes

433
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:01,000
and the patrol leaders council gets together for a bit

434
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:06,000
and I may be in the role of administrator again.

435
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
I may be in the role of manager,

436
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,000
But typically I'll be in the role of a coach.

437
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
I may ask them some questions,

438
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,000
I may try and help them find out

439
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
why something worked really well

440
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,000
or why something didn't work particularly well.

441
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,000
And all the time I'm trying to think of

442
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,000
the elements that are affecting the role that I'm playing.

443
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
Am I working from bias?

444
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
Am I trying to coerce them into something

445
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
that I want to see happen?

446
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
Am I using my authority properly or improperly?

447
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:43,000
Am I dispensing my responsibilities properly?

448
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
So that's a very broad, raging example

449
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
of what I'm talking about

450
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
In the roles that we play

451
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:54,000
within the volunteer positions that we occupy,

452
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:59,000
the assets and skills that we need to take to those roles

453
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
and the elements that influence them.

454
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
So in English, the scout motor is B-prepared

455
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
in Afrikaans.

456
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,000
In Afrikaans, we are coerced.

457
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Does Peter the Blue and Eliesta

458
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
on the Scoutmaster podcast?

459
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:38,000
That's right, it's time for a Scoutmaster's Minute.

460
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:51,000
I was a wrestling coach for- oh, a half a dozen years or so

461
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:54,000
And, like a lot of coaches,

462
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
I tended to get emotionally involved with our matches

463
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,000
Even more on the level of a player than a coach.

464
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
This could cut both ways.

465
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,000
I mean coaches who get viscerally and emotionally invested in your teams.

466
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:08,000
they can become a little temperamental.

467
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
they can become a little mercurial and barometric.

468
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:14,000
They'll focus on victory rather than building skills and teamwork.

469
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:18,000
because you know, if you've ever played a sport,

470
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
you know how intense it can be,

471
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
how intense game day can be.

472
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:23,000
If you've ever been a coach,

473
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:28,000
you know that you are going to share that same intensity with your players.

474
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:31,000
Now I had a fellow coach at the same school

475
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
who was much more experienced than I

476
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
and he had an analytical and calm approach that I really admired.

477
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:39,000
He could watch a match quietly

478
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
without getting all worked up and getting all crazy

479
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:44,000
and losing his voice like I did on occasion.

480
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,000
He could then take what he saw in that match

481
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,000
to a practice and develop skills and teamwork,

482
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
rather than create some cinematic moment

483
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
of the inspiring coaches lecture scene

484
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:00,000
that you know I gave a shot at a few times.

485
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:03,000
His quiet approach made the moments where he did speak to inspire

486
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:06,000
all the more meaningful and all the more powerful.

487
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
That coach had many more years of experience than I

488
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
and I began to see the reason and the intelligence

489
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
in his approach and I started changing the way I coached.

490
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
I was calmer, I was happier and I was more effective.

491
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
I learned that I could be fully invested in the team

492
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
without having to be emotional and over the top.

493
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Now, when I talked with this other coach, it was clear

494
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:30,000
he was no less frustrated, no less upset about things

495
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,000
or no less hopeful and no less emotionally invested than I was.

496
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:37,000
The difference was he had a longer view of the situation

497
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:40,000
and he'd develop useful ways to channel his energy

498
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:43,000
into actions that would build the team.

499
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:47,000
Scout leaders and coaches: both are invested in the success

500
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
of their players or scouts.

501
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:52,000
Driving them, threatening them, punishing them,

502
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:56,000
dragging them towards success is really tiresome

503
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:59,000
for players, for scouts, for coaches and scout masters.

504
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
It's almost impossible to maintain that level of energy

505
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
year in and year out.

506
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:07,000
Trying to do that makes a scout master touchy

507
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
and grumpy and unpleasant.

508
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,000
To my mind, the best thing I can do as a scout master

509
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
is listen and observe.

510
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:17,000
If I can listen and observe non-judgmentally,

511
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:20,000
like a disinterested third party, all the better.

512
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:23,000
When I'm doing this, I've hopefully really put the whole

513
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
thing in the hands of the scouts.

514
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:28,000
I'm not personally invested in the success or failure

515
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,000
of their efforts, I'm just observing.

516
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,000
Then I can advise, coach and mentor them

517
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
in a way that will really help.

518
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
If I'm participating emotionally,

519
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
I'm going to respond emotionally,

